    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_71bb0bdc69e8247ee6edd1a7929c51866e0768b238d06d6a72c2901adec79000",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source presents a reusable asymptotic-to-hyperbolic workflow: first choose a positive moment or coefficient representation that exposes a moving saddle, then turn its all-order asymptotics into local adjacent-ratio data, and finally use binomial finite differences to cancel lower-order drift and extract a Hermite limit. The modular-form calculation shows that a different global asymptotic representation can enter the same ratio interface.",
